Needed before the screens can switch to the real server
Staff management: list staff, add a staff member, and switch an account on or off. It needs GET /users, POST /users and PATCH /users/:id, plus GET /roles from your plan.
Clients (staff side): list clients, add a walk-in client, and look up a client by phone or email at checkout. It needs GET /customers, POST /customers and GET /customers/lookup. The customers table already exists from migration 002.
Client portal: sign up, sign in, sign out and “who am I” for clients. The client page also needs a public product list (no cost or stock counts) and a “my purchases” list.
The endpoints are /client/auth/signup, /client/auth/login, /client/auth/logout, /client/auth/me, /public/products and /client/purchases.
Clients need their own sign-in, separate from staff, with its own attempt limits.
Suppliers and purchase orders: a new migration 003 for suppliers, purchase orders and goods receipts. It needs GET and POST /suppliers, GET and POST /purchase-orders, and POST /purchase-orders/:id/receipts. Receiving goods also adds stock and updates the product cost, as in the demo.
Reports: server-side totals for a date range: daily sales, payment breakdown, sales by cashier, products sold, stock valuation, gross margin and low stock. Today the browser calculates these from the sales list, which becomes slow and incomplete with a lot of data. Your plan also lists GET /reports/inventory.
Connecting the screens:
Switch the screens off demo data 
Make the shift calls use the till ID from /auth/me instead of the placeholder.
Check every screen against the real responses.
Needed for real shops, not for the first switch-over
M-Pesa payment prompt: POST /payments and POST /payments/webhooks/mpesa. This sends the prompt to the customer’s phone and finishes the sale when Safaricom confirms. Today the cashier types the M-Pesa code by hand, and the server only checks it hasn’t been used twice, not that it’s real. This needs Safaricom Daraja credentials and a public web address.
Audit log viewer: GET /audit-logs. The data is already being recorded, but there’s no way yet for a manager to read it. Your definition of success says a manager can audit a transaction.
Cash in and out of the drawer: pay-ins and pay-outs during a shift. The table exists, but there are no endpoints or screens.
Settings: things that are fixed in code today, such as the 5% cashier discount limit, the receipt header and footer, and the business name.
Refund protection against double-clicks: checkout has it, but refunds don’t yet.
Product images in file storage: images are saved inside the database today. That’s fine to start but heavy for many products.
Password changes and resets, and locking an account after repeated bad attempts.
Production readiness
Automated tests with Jest or Vitest and Supertest, as in your plan, so the checks run on every change.
Deployment: Docker, managed PostgreSQL, HTTPS, backups with a tested restore, and error monitoring.
Kenya compliance: KRA tax invoicing (eTIMS), data-protection rules and approved payment services. Your document says to assess these before going live.
Optional later modules
Branch transfers and stock batches or expiry, plus the restaurant, pharmacy and other industry modules. Your plan keeps these out of the first release.
